'''Foralism''' ([[Castadillaan Pelaxian|Pelaxian]]: ''foralismo''), also known as '''rationalised feudalism''', '''semi-feudalism''', and '''Toryism''' in older [[Ænglish language|Ænglish]]-language sources, is the form of {{wp|traditionalist conservatism}} which governed the [[Viceroyalty of Los Rumas]] and the subsequent [[Delepasian Kingdom]] and [[Delepasian Confederation|Confederation]] from the fall of the [[First Pelaxian Republic]] in 1814 until the 1860s though lingering elements of the philosophy legally remained in place until 1888, and culturally until the rise of the ''[[Estado Social (Castadilla)|Estado Social]]'' in the 1920s. Foralists were staunch {{wp|monarchism|monarchists}}, supporters of the legal system of {{wp|common law}}, wished for the preservation of the traditional decentralised political structure of the Viceroyalty, and were greatly opposed to the {{wp|liberalism}} found in [[Delepasianism]].

